// 07-change-a-rule.dax
// A business rule changes. Which measures does it touch, and what moves?
// (Asked by Gustavo D. Silveira before the Barcelona session.)
//
// Start from a model where 01 and 03 were kept with "Update model with changes":
// AW.PerOrder is in the model, with Average Order Value and Lines per Order calling it.
// Step 1 and step 2 read the model and change nothing. Step 3 is the change itself.
//
// The example rule change: Finance now wants every "per order" figure rounded
// to 2 decimals, so the dashboard and the monthly pack show the same number.

// The new rule, as a query-only function with another name (used in step 2).
DEFINE
    FUNCTION AW.PerOrderNew = (
            amount : NUMERIC
        ) =>
        ROUND ( DIVIDE ( amount, DISTINCTCOUNT ( 'Sales Order'[Sales Order] ) ), 2 )

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 1 · Who calls the function?
// Measures whose expression mentions AW.PerOrder.
EVALUATE
SELECTCOLUMNS (
    FILTER ( INFO.VIEW.MEASURES (), CONTAINSSTRING ( [Expression], "AW.PerOrder" ) ),
    "Table", [Table],
    "Measure", [Name],
    "Expression", [Expression]
)

// The same from the dependency metadata. It also lists the second level:
// measures that call a measure that calls the function.
EVALUATE
FILTER (
    INFO.CALCDEPENDENCY (),
    [REFERENCED_OBJECT] = "AW.PerOrder"
        || [REFERENCED_OBJECT] IN { "Average Order Value", "Lines per Order" }
)
// If your Power BI Desktop version does not list functions in INFO.CALCDEPENDENCY,
// the first query is enough: functions are referenced by name in the expression.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 2 · Before and after, side by side, without touching the model.
// The new rule is AW.PerOrderNew, defined at the top of this file, only for this query.
EVALUATE
SUMMARIZECOLUMNS (
    'Date'[Fiscal Year],
    "AOV today", AW.PerOrder ( SUM ( Sales[Sales Amount] ) ),
    "AOV new rule", AW.PerOrderNew ( SUM ( Sales[Sales Amount] ) ),
    "Lines per order today", AW.PerOrder ( COUNTROWS ( Sales ) ),
    "Lines per order new rule", AW.PerOrderNew ( COUNTROWS ( Sales ) )
)
ORDER BY 'Date'[Fiscal Year]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 3 · Apply the change: one place, every measure follows.
// In model/functions.tmdl (or TMDL view), change only the body of AW.PerOrder:
//
//     ROUND ( DIVIDE ( amount, DISTINCTCOUNT ( 'Sales Order'[Sales Order] ) ), 2 )
//
// Commit before and after: the Git diff is one line, and the list from step 1
// is the list of measures to check. No measure is edited.
//
// Which reports use those measures?
// - Power BI Project (PBIR) in Git: search the report folder for the measure names,
//   for example:  git grep -l "Average Order Value" -- "*.Report"
// - Published model: in the Fabric or Power BI workspace, open the semantic model,
//   Lineage view or Impact analysis, to see the reports and dashboards built on it.
